  <title>Building Character: Judgment</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01538</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Building Character: Judgment</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c4692c78-5ce8-42f2-9036-20109faafa20</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Throughout the unit, you will be provided with resources to promote students’ character development. These resources come from <link url="https://characterlab.org/">Character Lab</link> and their <link url="https://characterlab.org/playbooks/">Playbooks</link>.</para>
<para>The focus for this unit is on <emphasis> judgment </emphasis>— the ability to make sound decisions without bias.</para>
<list>
  <item> In the Unit 6 Overview and Readiness lesson, students will see an example of how this trait is modeled through a real-world scenario. </item>
  <item> In each lesson’s Teacher Guide, you will be provided with links to tips that may help you foster this trait in students. </item>
</list>
<title>Why Does Judgment Matter?</title>
<para>Jugement is the ability to get as close as possible to the best answer without bias. The ability to separate personal beliefs from facts is a skill that needs to be developed. Being aware of personal bias and the ability to overcome bias makes for good judgment.</para>
<title>How Do I Encourage Judgment in My Students?</title>
<para>Consider some of these suggestions for promoting judgment in your classroom:</para>
<list>
  <item><emphasis>Model it. </emphasis>Talk about when you need to exercise judgment and when you don’t: “This is a problem where we should apply a predefined policy or set of guidelines, not a case-by-case judgment.” Where judgment is required, emphasize that disagreements are likely. Focus attention on the process: “What principles should we use to make this decision?”</item>
  <item><emphasis>Celebrate it.</emphasis> Praise people who are aware of the limits of their own judgment: “It’s great that you are trying hard to get the right answer, not just sticking to your prior beliefs.”</item>
  <item><emphasis>Enable it.</emphasis> Help break decisions into bite-size chunks: “What are the smaller, easier considerations on which this judgment depends, and how can you make them separately?” Use comparisons: Relative judgments are more reliable than absolute ones. Ask questions that take an “outside view”: “What would the typical answer be for comparable problems?” Leverage the diversity of perspectives: “Can we obtain independent opinions and use their average?”</item>
</list></section>
